From: Alex <xnderv@yandex.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/nouveau/disp: don't refuse HDMI when SCDC is unsupported below 340 MHz

nvkm_uoutp_mthd_hdmi() returns -EINVAL whenever the sink advertises SCDC
but the SOR has no hdmi->scdc hook (everything before GM200). The caller,
nv50_hdmi_enable(), then bails out, so HDMI mode is never enabled and no
AVI/vendor InfoFrames are sent: the output silently degrades to DVI-style
TMDS. This happens with any HDMI 2.x monitor (e.g. 2560x1440@60, 241.5 MHz)
on Fermi/Kepler. SCDC is only required above 340 MHz (TMDS bit-clock ratio
1/40 and scrambling); below that the sink must accept a plain HDMI 1.4
signal, and nv50_hdmi_enable() already clears scrambling over DDC.

Only refuse when the requested clock actually needs SCDC.

Note (2026-10-22): fixed independently upstream by commit 1717fcc5be57 ("drm/nouveau/disp:
don't reject HDMI config on cards without SCDC", Giuseppe Ranieri, 2026-09-17), which drops
the SCDC term altogether. This patch is kept for 7.0.x kernels; prepare.sh skips it once the
upstream fix is present (see 0003-*.patch.needs).

Seen on GF114 (GTX 560 Ti) + MSI MPG271QX OLED:
  [HDMI head:0 enable:1 ... khz:241500 scdc:1 scdc_scrambling:1 scdc_low_rates:0] (ret:-22)

---
diff --git a/nvkm/engine/disp/uoutp.c b/nvkm/engine/disp/uoutp.c
--- a/nvkm/engine/disp/uoutp.c
+++ b/nvkm/engine/disp/uoutp.c
@@ -254,7 +254,7 @@
 	if (!ior->func->hdmi ||
 	    args->v0.max_ac_packet > 0x1f ||
 	    args->v0.rekey > 0x7f ||
-	    (args->v0.scdc && !ior->func->hdmi->scdc))
+	    (args->v0.scdc && !ior->func->hdmi->scdc && args->v0.khz > 340000))
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	if (!args->v0.enable) {
